usatst:2024:4.text:
Find all integers $n \ge 2$ for which there exists a sequence of $2n$ pairwise distinct points $(P_1, \dots, P_n, Q_1, \dots, Q_n)$ in the plane satisfying the following four conditions:

 (1) no three of the $2n$ points are collinear; 
 (2) $P_iP_{i+1} \ge 1$ for all $i=1,2,\dots,n$, where $P_{n+1} = P_1$; 
 (3) $Q_iQ_{i+1} \ge 1$ for all $i=1,2,\dots,n$, where $Q_{n+1} = Q_1$; and 
 (4) $P_i Q_j \leq 1$ for all $i=1,2,\dots,n$ and $j=1,2,\dots,n$.

Proposed by Ray Li.

usatst:2023:3.text:
Consider pairs $(f,g)$ of functions from the set of nonnegative integers to itself such that

 • $f(0) \ge f(1) \ge f(2) \ge \dots \ge f(300) \ge 0$; 
 • $f(0) + f(1) + f(2) + \dots + f(300) \leq 300$; 
 • for any $20$ nonnegative integers $n_1$, $n_2$, …, $n_{20}$, not necessarily distinct, we have \[ g(n_1 + n_2 + \dots + n_{20}) \leq f(n_1) + f(n_2) + \dots + f(n_{20}). \]

 Determine the maximum possible value of $g(0) + g(1) + \dots + g(6000)$ over all such pairs of functions.

Proposed by Sean Li.

usatst:2001:3.text:
For a set $S$, let $|S|$ denote the number of elements in $S$. Let $A$ be a set of positive integers with $|A| = 2001$. Prove that there exists a set $B$ such that

(i) $B \subseteq A$;

(ii) $|B| \ge 668$;

(iii) for any $u, v \in B$ (not necessarily distinct), $u+v \not\in B$.

usatst:2003:2.text:
Let $ABC$ be a triangle and let $P$ be a point in its interior. Lines $PA$, $PB$, $PC$ intersect sides $BC$, $CA$, $AB$ at $D$, $E$, $F$, respectively. Prove that

\[ [PAF]+[PBD]+[PCE]=\frac{1}{2}[ABC]  \]

if and only if $P$ lies on at least one of the medians of triangle $ABC$. (Here $[XYZ]$ denotes the area of triangle $XYZ$.)

usatst:2005:1.text:
Let $n$ be an integer greater than $1$. For a positive integer $m$, let $S_{m}= \{ 1,2,\ldots, mn\}$. Suppose that there exists a $2n$-element set $T$ such that

(a) each element of $T$ is an $m$-element subset of $S_{m}$;

(b) each pair of elements of $T$ shares at most one common element;

and

(c) each element of $S_{m}$ is contained in exactly two elements of $T$.

Determine the maximum possible value of $m$ in terms of $n$.
